protocolprobe-selftest: dual-use disclosure

What it is
A command-line tool that runs ProtocolProbe's MCP server checks against an MCP server that YOU run, from YOUR machine:
either a server it starts on your machine (--stdio), or, from 0.2.0, your own Streamable HTTP server at a URL (--url), but
only after that server has shown, by returning a one-time value, that you control it. It connects as an MCP client, lists
the server's tools, calls them, and reports what it saw.

Dual-use functionality
Two checks behave like a security test and are therefore off unless you turn them on:
  --allow-mutating  calls tools whose names or annotations suggest they change data (a tool may really change data)
  --allow-fuzzing   sends wrong-type, oversized and missing-required input to tools
The remaining checks call tools with normal-shaped arguments, start a listener on 127.0.0.1 to see whether a tool fetches a
URL it is given, and read tool descriptions, resources and prompts.

Intended legitimate use
A developer or operator checking their own MCP server before release, or in their own CI.

What keeps it to that
- --i-own-this-server must be typed on every run.
- --stdio: a command that contains a non-local http(s) address is refused, so the stdio lane cannot be pointed at a remote
  server through a proxy.
- --url: a URL is accepted only after the server at that exact URL returns a one-time value from this tool:
    - the first run makes a value (256 random bits), prints it, keeps only its hash on your machine, and connects to nothing;
    - you add the line "protocolprobe-proof=<value>" on its own line to your server's MCP instructions (the text it returns
      at initialize) and run the same command again within 30 minutes;
    - the value is read on the same connection that then runs the checks, before any tool is listed or called; it works
      once (it is deleted before the checks start) and only for that exact URL (scheme, host, port, path and query);
    - no redirect is followed, of any kind; the host name is resolved once and every connection of the run goes to that
      address, so a DNS change during the run is not followed; https certificates are checked against the host name;
    - https for any host; http only for this machine (localhost, 127.0.0.1, ::1, and the name must resolve to loopback);
    - a server that needs a credential (0.3.0): you may give your OWN credential,
      only through an environment variable: --bearer-env VAR sends "Authorization: Bearer <value of VAR>",
      --header-env Name=VAR sends that header.
      There is no option that takes the value itself (shell history and the process list would show it). The value is
      sent only to the exact proven URL, on the session that reads the proof and runs the checks; never on a redirect
      (none is followed), never to OAuth metadata (same host or not) and never to the one initialize the tool sends
      WITHOUT credentials to see whether your server also accepts a session with none. It is never printed, never
      written to --out and never stored: its exact value (as is, JSON-escaped and URL-encoded) is replaced by
      [redacted] in everything the tool prints and writes, also when your server repeats it in an error or a result.
      A server that refuses the credential is not tested (exit 4, "your server refused the credential you supplied").
      Headers that frame or route the request, hop-by-hop headers, cookies and the MCP transport's own headers cannot be
      set. A credential is not the proof: the one-time value is still required;
    - OAuth metadata (checks 14-18) is read only on the server's own origin; an authorization server on another host is
      not contacted. Check 16 (redirect_uri probing) is never run;
    - Streamable HTTP only, not the older SSE transport.
- Time limits: --connect-timeout (default 180 s) bounds the start or connect, --max-time (default 600 s) bounds the whole
  run. When a limit is reached the server is stopped (stdio) or the connection closed (URL), and no result is written. On
  Windows, a stdio server still running 2 s after its input closes has its whole process tree ended with the system's
  taskkill command (only the process this tool started).
- When a stdio server cannot start, the last lines of its own error output are printed locally, with the planted canary
  value and secret-looking values hidden. For a URL, an HTTP error shows its status only, never the response body.
- Results are printed and, with --out, written to a file on your machine. The tool sends nothing to ProtocolProbe or anyone
  else; ProtocolProbe runs and hosts nothing for it. Its score is "N of M checks passed" over the checks that ran; checks
  that did not run, or do not apply over HTTP, are listed and never counted.
- 0.4.0, for CI: --fail-on-finding only changes the exit code (5 when a check needs attention). --sarif writes the result
  as a SARIF file on your machine, with the same redaction as --out and without your command line; uploading it anywhere
  (for example to GitHub code scanning) is your own step.
- 0.5.0: after your server lists its tools, the rules we measured for what Anthropic's, OpenAI's and Google's APIs accept
  in a tool list are run on that list offline (no network, no model call, nothing sent to any vendor). A match says the
  list may be refused by that API; no match is not a guarantee. It is not part of the score, the SARIF file or the exit code.
- 0.5.1: the requests the command sends to your server's address and to its OAuth metadata carry the User-Agent
  ProtocolProbe/1.0 (+https://protocolprobe.com; hello@protocolprobe.com), so your logs show who sent them. A remote server
  that asks for roots gets an empty list instead of the path of a temporary folder on your machine. No check's status or
  wording changed.

Known limit (stated plainly)
The proof stops accidents and casual misuse. It is not enforcement: this tool is open source, so anyone can change it to
skip the proof, and other tools connect to any URL with no proof at all. The proof shows that whoever runs the command
controls what the server at that URL answers in its instructions, not that they control the platform under it, and not
that they may test it under that platform's terms. A server that copies text from other people into its instructions, or
a gateway that passes on another server's instructions, could be proven by someone who does not run it; this cannot be
detected in general. Use --url only for a server you operate or are authorised to test.
The --stdio address check reads http(s) addresses in the command line only. A wrapper script that reaches someone else's
server, or a host written without a scheme, is not detected. Do not use this tool against a server you do not own or have
permission to test.

Over HTTP (--url)
The server is not a process this tool starts. Checks 4 (secret leak) and 11 (log leak) look for a fake secret planted in
such a process's environment, so over HTTP they are N-A with the reason and never counted. Checks 5 (requests to a URL it
is given) and 10 (reads outside the folder it was given) use a listener and files on this machine: they run when the URL's
host is localhost, 127.0.0.1 or ::1, and are N-A for any other host. Without a credential, checks 14 and 15 are N-A for a
server that accepts a session without one. With your credential they are NOT-TESTABLE and say what is missing: 14 (token
passthrough) needs an upstream service we can watch receive the token, 15 (token audience) needs a second token issued for
another audience; this tool has neither and makes up neither. The result says, as a fact, whether your server also opened
a session for the initialize sent without credentials. Check 16 is never run.
Known limit of the redaction: a server that returns the credential in another form (for example base64-encoded, split, or
reversed) is not recognised; such output would be printed and written as the server sent it. Keep --out files private.

Through a proxy (--stdio, 0.1.2)
Through a stdio-to-HTTP proxy (for example npx mcp-remote http://localhost:3000/mcp) the process this tool starts is the
proxy, not your server. Checks 4 (secret leak) and 11 (log leak) look for a fake secret planted in that process's
environment, so through a proxy they are reported N-A with the reason and never counted; 0.1.1 counted check 4 as passed
there. Checks 5 and 10 still run when the command carries the local address (the server is on this machine), and are N-A
when a known proxy is named with no local address. Detected: a command naming mcp-remote, supergateway, mcp-proxy or
@pyroprompts/mcp-stdio-to-streamable-http-adapter, or carrying a local http(s) address. Not detected: any other proxy, a
wrapper script around one, or a server inside a container or virtual machine (not tested); there checks 4, 5, 10 and 11 can
come out clean without meaning anything. A local HTTP server is best tested with --url http://localhost:<port>/<path>, or
by starting it in stdio mode if its framework allows.
